{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development&nbsp;results in a loss of a large&nbsp;family-sized unit which is a priority house type within the Borough.&nbsp;As such, the proposal is considered unacceptable and contrary to the following:Policy BC4 of the Borough Wide DPD (March 2011)Policies SP 3 and DMH 5 of the&nbsp;Draft Local Plan Regulation 19 Main Modifications Version (February 2024).",
    "The proposed development would result in sub-standard housing, failing to provide adequate internal space, especially in terms of communal living areas, to ensure a good quality of accommodation for a 4-bed, 4-person HMO, creating a cramped and uncomfortable living environment. Additionally,&nbsp; bedrooms 1 and 2 do not meet the minimum size requirements for single bedrooms. In particular, bedroom 2 is excessively narrow and long, with its only light source being an inaccessible skylight, which further exacerbates the sense of confinement and overcrowding. As such the proposal is therefore considered to be unacceptbale and contrary to:Policy D6 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policy BP6 of the Borough Wide DPD (March 2011)DCLG Technical Housing Standards (nationally described space standard) (DCLG, March 2015) (as amended)Housing Design Standards London Planning Guidance (June 2023)",
    "The proposed HMO has the potential to increase activity level and coming and goings to and from the site leading to additional levels of noise and disturbance at the property. This is considered to have a negative impact on neighbouring amenity in this street of family housing and in turn the health and wellbeing of neighbouring residents. As such the proposal is contrary to the following:Policies BP8 and BP11 of the Borough Wide DPD (March 2011).Policies DMD 1, SP 7 and DMSI 3 of the Draft Local Plan Regulation 19 Main Modifications Version (February 2024).",
    "The proposal fails to adequately demonstrate that the development would not result in an increase in&nbsp; parking pressure along First Avenue and the wider area. As such the proposal is contrary to the following:Policy T6 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policy DMT 2&nbsp; of the&nbsp;Draft Local Plan Regulation 19 Main Modifications Version (February 2024)."
  ],
